WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.520
In a simple model, the indirect route arrives later.

2
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.780
What does a slower report cost, and what does it add?

3
00:00:10.733 --> 00:00:13.173
Take illustrative numbers for a mouse hindpaw.

4
00:00:14.013 --> 00:00:18.513
In this model, the indirect input arrives about 4.5 milliseconds later.

5
00:00:19.517 --> 00:00:23.417
That lag is a model, not a measurement, and its size is uncertain.

6
00:00:24.317 --> 00:00:30.517
With equal paths from cord to brainstem, matched final synapses and a PSDC axon no

7
00:00:30.517 --> 00:00:33.137
faster, the spinal synapses make it later.

8
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.400
Pause and predict.

9
00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:40.540
Does a lag of a few milliseconds matter more for a long press, or for fast

10
00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:41.540
vibration?

11
00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:51.710
For the steady firing during a 300 millisecond press, it may matter little.

12
00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:56.770
The lag is a sliver at the start, though onset timing is a separate question.

13
00:00:57.783 --> 00:01:01.083
At 500 hertz, one cycle lasts 2 milliseconds.

14
00:01:02.143 --> 00:01:03.943
The lag spans more than two whole cycles.

15
00:01:05.017 --> 00:01:07.957
A fixed delay does not blur locking on its own route.

16
00:01:08.777 --> 00:01:12.097
Every spike is equally late, so the spikes still share one phase.

17
00:01:12.977 --> 00:01:15.077
That phase shifts, by a quarter cycle here.

18
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.640
What blurs timing is jitter.

19
00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:22.780
In this toy model, each synapse adds random scatter to when the next spike fires.

20
00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:28.430
With assumed jitter, two synapses nearly erase locking at 500 hertz.

21
00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:31.470
At 50 hertz, locking barely changes.

22
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.040
That model is not the paper's explanation.

23
00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:40.560
But it shows how relay jitter could explain why fast timing rides the route with no

24
00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:41.560
relay.

25
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:46.260
Now a second question.

26
00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:48.620
What does the cord do to the signal?

27
00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:53.900
PSDC neurons get most of their excitation from local interneurons.

28
00:01:54.883 --> 00:02:00.903
single dorsal horn neurons received input from both low-threshold and high-threshold

29
00:02:00.903 --> 00:02:02.043
mechanoreceptors.

30
00:02:02.933 --> 00:02:05.173
Two kinds of inhibition shape the output.

31
00:02:06.053 --> 00:02:11.413
In feedforward inhibition, the afferent excites the PSDC neuron and an inhibitory

32
00:02:11.413 --> 00:02:12.413
interneuron.

33
00:02:12.967 --> 00:02:16.347
That interneuron then inhibits the PSDC neuron.

34
00:02:17.227 --> 00:02:20.447
Here, Rorβ interneurons supplied it, using glycine.

35
00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:26.600
Presynaptic inhibition lands on the afferent terminal itself, and scales down the

36
00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:27.660
transmitter it releases.

37
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.060
Pause and predict.

38
00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:32.460
Remove either kind of inhibition.

39
00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.300
Does the PSDC receptive field shrink, or grow?

40
00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:48.380
It grows. And losing presynaptic inhibition left neurons firing after the

41
00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:49.380
indentation ended.

42
00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:53.340
These were chronic knockouts, recorded under anaesthesia.

43
00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:57.780
And no study here recorded these neurons in an awake animal.

44
00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.280
The takeaway: two routes, one patch of skin.

45
00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:03.160
One keeps the timing.

46
00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:06.520
The other, rebuilt in the cord, reports the pressure.
